@using CoupleOS.Application.Capture
@using CoupleOS.Domain.Enums
@model CaptureReport

@*
    The change report, and the one screen M2 was built to make honest.

    It is rendered from the blocks, not from the actions. The M0 version listed
    what the run did, so a line the model ignored produced no line here and
    disappeared — an event, on the first real run, seen by nobody. Every block
    the run touched appears below whatever became of it, and the ones it did not
    touch are counted, so the numbers at the bottom always add up to the file.

    Refusals are as prominent as successes. SPEC.md 46 forbids success language
    over a failed action, and a refusal the user never sees is indistinguishable
    from the system losing their note.
*@

@if (Model.NothingToRead)
{
    @* Not "the file is empty": after a run has cleared the inbox behind itself,
       the usual way to reach zero is a file full of archive with nothing new in
       front of it. *@
    <p class="muted">Nothing to read — there is nothing in the file that a run has not already handled.</p>
}
else if (Model.NothingToDo)
{
    @* "Processed by an earlier run" is only true of the blocks that succeeded.
       Since the rewrite files those out of the inbox, what is usually left in
       front of a couple who are up to date is precisely the failures — and
       calling those processed is success language over a failed action, which
       SPEC.md 46 forbids. So the two are separate sentences. *@
    @if (Model.Stranded < Model.BlocksSeen)
    {
        <p class="muted">
            Nothing new. @(Model.BlocksSeen - Model.Stranded)
            @(Model.BlocksSeen - Model.Stranded == 1 ? "block" : "blocks")
            in the file were handled by an earlier run.
        </p>
    }
}

@if (Model.Stranded > 0)
{
    @* Nothing retries a failed block (STATUS debt 24), so the line sits in the
       inbox looking outstanding while every press of Process ignores it. Saying
       so, and saying what to do about it, is the difference between a limitation
       and a line quietly going nowhere. *@
    <p class="warning">
        @if (Model.Stranded == 1)
        {
            <text>One line in the file failed on an earlier run and will not be
            retried on its own. Edit it and press Process again.</text>
        }
        else
        {
            <text>@Model.Stranded lines in the file failed on an earlier run and
            will not be retried on their own. Edit them and press Process again.</text>
        }
    </p>
}

@*
    The buckets render beside that warning, not instead of it. This was the else
    of the block above, which meant one line that failed on an earlier run hid
    everything the current run had just done — including a question waiting to be
    answered. The two facts are independent: the file has old failures in it, and
    this run handled some blocks.

    Named "bucket" rather than "section": @section is a Razor directive, and
    @section.Heading is a compile error rather than a property access.
*@
@foreach (var bucket in new[]
{
    (Status: DumpBlockStatus.Processed,  Heading: "added"),
    (Status: DumpBlockStatus.NeedsInput, Heading: "needs your input"),
    (Status: DumpBlockStatus.Ignored,    Heading: "read, nothing to do"),
    (Status: DumpBlockStatus.Failed,     Heading: "not applied"),
})
{
    var included = Model.With(bucket.Status).ToList();

    if (included.Count == 0)
    {
        continue;
    }

    <h2>@bucket.Heading</h2>

    @if (bucket.Status == DumpBlockStatus.NeedsInput)
    {
        @* The questions are also in the file, under a heading of the same name,
           and that is where they get answered — either partner, in their own
           time (ADR 0009). Saying so here is what makes the parking legible as
           a queue rather than as a run that half-failed. *@
        <p class="muted">
            These are waiting in the file under <em>Needs your input</em>.
            Add a line below each one with the answer and press Process again.
        </p>
    }

    <ul class="report">
        @foreach (var block in included)
        {
            <li>
                @* The user's own words first. A report phrased only in the
                   system's vocabulary makes the reader hunt for which line it is
                   talking about. *@
                <span class="block-text">@block.RawText</span>

                @if (block.Changes.Count > 0)
                {
                    <ul class="changes">
                        @foreach (var change in block.Changes)
                        {
                            <li class="@(change.Outcome == CoupleOS.Application.Tools.ToolOutcome.Success ? "" : "warning")">
                                @change.Description
                            </li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                }
                else if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(block.Note))
                {
                    <span class="muted note">@block.Note</span>
                }
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
}

@*
    Handled and in-the-file are two different numbers and are printed as two,
    because they genuinely differ: a run also picks up blocks an earlier run
    abandoned, and those belong to text that may since have been edited away.
    Printing only the file's count next to a longer list is how a tally starts
    lying.
*@
<p class="muted tally">
    @Model.Blocks.Count handled
    · @Model.BlocksSeen @(Model.BlocksSeen == 1 ? "block" : "blocks") in the file
    @* Minus the stranded ones. AlreadyRecorded means "the file already had this
       block", which is not the same as "it worked" — the failures are in that
       number too, and they are called out above. Printing them here as well would
       both double-count them and call them processed. *@
    @if (Model.AlreadyRecorded - Model.Stranded > 0)
    {
        <text>· @(Model.AlreadyRecorded - Model.Stranded) already processed</text>
    }
    @if (Model.Stranded > 0)
    {
        <text>· @Model.Stranded failed earlier</text>
    }
    @if (Model.Usage is { } usage)
    {
        <text>
            · @usage.Model, @usage.Calls @(usage.Calls == 1 ? "call" : "calls")
            · @usage.PromptTokens in, @usage.CompletionTokens out
            · @usage.Duration.TotalSeconds.ToString("0.0")s
        </text>
    }
</p>
